            <ArticleTitle>The headache of a hyperactive calcium channel.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>653-4</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.neuron.2009.02.015</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Migraine is thought to be triggered by excessive neocortical neuronal excitability that leads to cortical spreading depression. In this issue of Neuron, Tottene et al. study a mouse model of familial hemiplegic migraine type 1, and provide evidence for the hyperactivity of P/Q-type calcium channel-mediated cortical glutamatergic synaptic transmission as an underlying mechanism for the susceptibility of cortical spreading depression initiation in migraine disorders.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
